WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.628
Teacher midterm practice, question 27.

2
00:00:04.248 --> 00:00:08.215
True or false: dislocations slip relatively easily at room

3
00:00:08.215 --> 00:00:12.501
temperature in metallic and ionic materials, but cannot move in

4
00:00:12.501 --> 00:00:17.555
covalent materials, because covalent bonds are strongly directional.

5
00:00:19.255 --> 00:00:23.296
Metals: yes. Their bonds are non directional, and slip is easy.

6
00:00:23.916 --> 00:00:28.436
Ionic solids: no. Slip would bring ions of the same charge next to

7
00:00:28.436 --> 00:00:31.488
each other, and the repulsion resists it.

8
00:00:31.488 --> 00:00:33.719
So slip is not relatively easy.

9
00:00:34.339 --> 00:00:38.955
The covalent part is right, but the word ionic makes the whole statement false.

10
00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:42.452
So the answer is B: false.

11
00:00:43.271 --> 00:00:47.757
Do not group ionic with metallic just because both are non directional.

12
00:00:47.757 --> 00:00:50.173
Charge repulsion still blocks slip.
